{"name":"Breaking the Ice","hex":"#b8e4f5","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breaking the ice","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of break the ice"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breaking_the_ice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breaking the Ice (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Breaking the Ice is a 1938 American drama film directed by Edward F. Cline starring child star Bobby Breen. A young Mennonite boy runs away from home to earn money for his widowed mother."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4e/Breaking_the_Ice_%281938_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breaking_the_Ice_(film)"},{"title":"Frasier season 2","blocks":[{"text":"The second season of the American television sitcom Frasier aired on NBC from September 20, 1994 to May 23, 1995."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f2/Frasier_S2_DVD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frasier_season_2"},{"title":"Breaking the Ice (Star Trek: Enterprise)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Breaking the Ice\" is the eighth episode of the television series Star Trek: Enterprise, and was written by Maria Jacquemetton and Andre Jacquemetton. Terry Windell served as director for the episode. The episode aired on UPN on November 7, 2001. It was nominated for the 2002 Emmy Award for visual effects."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breaking_the_Ice_(Star_Trek%3A_Enterprise)"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/1280px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The three-dimensional crystal structure of H2O ice Ih (c) is composed of bases of H2O ice molecules (b) located on lattice points within the two-dimensional hexagonal space lattice (a).[4][5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIce_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG/500px-Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG/1280px-Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG/500px-Frozen_Wappinger_Creek.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frozen waterfall in southeast New York","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrozen_Wapping